  <data name="msg_about" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This app is capable of carrying scientific calculations in different steps with output in handwriting style. It supports the basic matrix operations, statistical calculations, complex operation and so on.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="msg_about_copyright" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Copyright © 2015-2021 by Howard Zhang</value>
  </data>
  <data name="msg_about_tail" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>If you like this App or have any questions about it, please go to the App store to rate it or write a feedback. Thank you!</value>
  </data>
  <data name="msg_about_version" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Version: </value>
  </data>
  <data name="msg_help_content_1" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Use brackets to represent matrix. Use comma to separate the elements in the row inside the matrix. Use two consecutive commas to separate each line. E.g. Enter (6,2,,3,1) represents a two-by-two matrix.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="msg_help_content_2_1" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>1, nCr: combinations,  "A nCr B" represents the number of ways of obtaining an unordered subset of B elements from a set of A elements.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="msg_help_content_2_2" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>2, nPr: permutations, "A nPr B" represents the number of ways of obtaining an ordered subset of B elements from a set of A elements.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="msg_help_content_2_3" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>3, asin: arcsin, acos, atan empathy.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="msg_help_content_2_4" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>4, Abs: for example "Abs n" represents the absolute value of n. It can also be used in matrix form, such as "Abs (6,2,,3,1)", which means a two-by-two determinant.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="msg_help_content_2_5" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>5, T: used for matrices, such as "(6,2,,3,1)^T", which means the transpose of a matrix (6,2,,3,1).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="msg_help_content_2_6" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>6, rank: used for matrices, such as "r(6,2,,3,1)", which means the rank of a matrix (6,2,,3,1).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="msg_help_content_2_7" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>7, Ex: average of several numbers. Input combined with matrix form, for example, "e (1,2,3,4)" means average number of 1, 2, 3, 4.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="msg_help_content_3" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Use the i button to enter the imaginary unit. Complex numbers support arithmetic, modulus (Abs), and positive, negative, fractional or complex powers. Fractional and complex powers use the principal value. Matrix powers require an integer exponent; negative exponents require an invertible square matrix.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="msg_help_content_4" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>You can enter a constant in the left corner of the keypad (with three butoons next to each other). Where e is the natural base, π is PI, I is the imaginary unit.</value>
  </data>
